<section image-layout="blank" id="PLOK1txAcZ" vertical-align="end" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.5" card-margin-hidden="true"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/-PiJn2wrxULvGn9iJL8gb.png" saved-media-id="6tuagej0s4e9nfu" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="practical-light-afterimage" alt="High-contrast editorial studio photograph of anonymous hands binding a stack of blank bone-white paper with one taut flare-coral thread under a single hard aperture light, controlled long-exposure afterimage, deep near-black negative space across the left side, restrained film grain, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="8Gy5JOJHOx" vertical-align="end"><p role="eyebrow">Creative conference talk</p><h1 font-size="64pt" style="padding-right: 26%;">The Vow of Chastity</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 38%;">Why your best work is waiting behind a rule you haven't written yet.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="5HWqnKlzE2" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="c7bC6breRc" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md" font-size="120pt">$50</display><h4>The wager</h4><p>Bennett Cerf of Random House bet against a vocabulary of 50 different words or fewer.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="qjENwhdmzT" vertical-align="center" colspan="40"><h2>In 1960, a publisher bet Dr. Seuss fifty dollars</h2><p>Theodor Geisel agonized, then produced <i>Green Eggs and Ham</i>, published 12 August 1960. Forty-nine of its words are monosyllables. The exception is “anywhere.”</p><p>By some estimates, it has sold north of 200 million copies, his best-selling book ever. Nobody remembers the books he wrote with unlimited vocabulary.</p><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://www.cbc.ca/lite/story/1.5686697">CBC</a> · <a href="https://brianjayjones.com/2020/08/11/sixty-years-of-green-eggs-and-ham/">Brian Jay Jones</a> · <a href="https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/green-eggs-and-ham/">Snopes</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="S9o8asbwaJ"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="FG0JUCBbFy" vertical-align="center" colspan="38"><h1 font-size="44pt">And yet we hand people a blank page and call it a gift</h1><p>The default studio brief leaves scope, budget and deliverable open, then says “surprise us.” We treat the immovable client, fixed format and inherited palette as damage.</p><p>Creative freedom is often an unmade decision handed downstream. The constraint is not the obstacle to the work. It is the instrument.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="0vD3fo3Hw0" vertical-align="start" cell-style="outline" padding="26pt" colspan="22"><p role="eyebrow">The blank page</p><labels><label variant="outline">Open scope</label><label variant="outline">Open budget</label><label variant="outline">No fixed deliverable</label></labels><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><h3>Unmade decisions, handed downstream.</h3></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ycwP_RdG_O" vertical-align="center" background-color="accent"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="GaymYImZuh" vertical-align="center"><p role="eyebrow">Section 01</p><h1 font-size="48pt" style="padding-right: 18%;">01 — What the evidence actually says</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 36%;">The boundary itself appears to be doing more work than researchers expected.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="9id00IMsbg" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="aD4sFuuQfk" vertical-align="center" colspan="19"><display size="md" font-size="128pt">111</display><h4>Published and unpublished studies</h4></grid-item><grid-item id="GPS-BXRLI5" vertical-align="center" colspan="41"><h2>111 studies disagree with your instincts</h2><p>A 2022 meta-analysis in the <i>Journal of Organizational Behavior</i> found a significant <b>positive</b> relationship between constraints and creative performance.</p><p>The type of constraint mattered less than researchers assumed. Much of the older disagreement traced to methodology, not the constraints themselves. The boundary itself is doing the work.</p><p role="caption">Source: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/job.2655">2022 meta-analysis, Journal of Organizational Behavior</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="_nj35qrTYO" vertical-align="end" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.64" card-margin-hidden="true"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/iV7nD48XtjAfNzPYQBwW0.png" saved-media-id="ngf3vckr3q8mbf5" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="practical-light-afterimage" alt="High-contrast editorial studio photograph of an anonymous hand abruptly placing a rigid coral frame across a flowing path of translucent paper prototypes, one hard practical light revealing a new opening beyond the interruption, deep near-black field on the left, controlled amber afterimage, restrained grain, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="09XV8sDMpq" vertical-align="end"><h1 font-size="42pt" style="padding-right: 28%;">The finding that should annoy you most</h1><p style="padding-right: 28%;">A Stanford study added a specified target audience and ad size at the beginning, middle or end of graphic-ad prototyping. <b>Late constraints produced the most novel results.</b></p><p style="padding-right: 28%;">A second experiment induced design fixation. Higher fixation also yielded more novel results. The 11th-hour scope change you dread may be the most generative event in the project.</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://stacks.stanford.edu/file/druid:tf829zy3088/Zhou-BoundingCreativity.pdf">Source: Stanford, “Bounding Creativity”</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="wnQefgkI3h"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="elGGwQynGP"><h2>Harder was better, even when it was worse</h2><p>A 2023 <i>Creativity Research Journal</i> study asked participants to write short sentences under low or high constraint on the initial letters they could use.</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="gIJpZsw7vr" vertical-align="center" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">What got worse</p><h3>Lower hit rate</h3><p>Under high constraint, participants were less likely to succeed at all.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="7LDwAIdoPe" background-color="accent" vertical-align="center" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">What got better</p><h3>Higher creative ceiling</h3><p>The sentences they did produce were judged more creative.</p></grid-item></grid><h3 role="subheading">Tight rules lower your hit rate and raise your ceiling. Plan for more failed attempts, not fewer.</h3><p role="caption"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10400419.2023.2182038">Source: Creativity Research Journal, 2023</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="_GBKW5nDC7"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="yNhWCTDqr1"><h2>But constraint is a dose, not a virtue</h2><p>Organizational research proposes that constraints on the problem and on resources have to be read together.</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="eGyr6cgdcZ" vertical-align="center" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Too little on both</p><h3>Ambiguous opportunity</h3><p>Nobody knows what the work is, so nothing gets made.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Rr89FXAIAV" background-color="palette-2" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Balanced</p><h3>Creative search</h3><p>Intrinsic motivation and creative search both stay switched on.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="EMK-rT-34t" vertical-align="center" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Too much on both</p><h3>Futile effort</h3><p>Everyone knows what the work is and nobody can reach it.</p></grid-item></grid><h3 role="subheading">The skill is not adding limits. It is tuning them.</h3><p role="caption"><a href="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/20413866231202031">Source: organizational research on multidimensional constraints</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="GFyb78Z4pv" vertical-align="center" background-color="palette-2"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="-IrIKmaJFl" vertical-align="center"><p role="eyebrow">Section 02</p><h1 font-size="52pt" style="padding-right: 24%;">02 — Why it works</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 38%;">Exclusion narrows the field. Focus gives the search a direction.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="tVw7Zn3tAZ"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="5JnBlu3pXw"><h2>Two kinds of rules, and only one of them helps</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="B8z0eXOLXg" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Exclusionary</p><h3>“No stock photography.”</h3><p>The rule clears the table. It ends an argument, but it does not tell you where to look next.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="4AV_p1ANit" background-color="palette-2" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Focusing</p><h3>“Every image must be something a customer could have shot on their own phone.”</h3><p>The rule excludes the same options and aims the search at a specific region of the problem.</p></grid-item></grid><h3 role="subheading">Read your rule aloud. If it only ends arguments, rewrite it until it also starts one.</h3><p role="caption"><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1871187122001870">Source: research on exclusionary and focusing constraints</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="0ibaJfgrZ4"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="fbK3XnYHwg" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/U32uSKXSRMaoRsXj90B7O.png" saved-media-id="m1ktv2wvh30mskk" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="practical-light-afterimage" alt="High-contrast editorial studio photograph of anonymous hands signing a stark manifesto beside a handheld film camera and a bare location slate, one practical hard light, bone paper against a near-black table, restrained amber afterimage and grain, all writing illegible, no logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item><grid-item id="WqLtGBOGad" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><h2>Filmmakers wrote theirs down and signed it</h2><p>On 13 March 1995 in Copenhagen, Lars von Trier and Thomas Vinterberg published the Dogme 95 manifesto and its ten-rule “Vow of Chastity.”</p><p>Shoot on location. No sound produced apart from the images. Camera handheld. No optical work or filters. No superficial action. The film takes place here and now. <b>The director must not be credited.</b></p><blockquote><p>“Discipline is the answer... we must put our films into uniform.”</p></blockquote><p>Every rule governs method. None governs meaning.</p><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://as.vanderbilt.edu/koepnick/WorldCinema_f02/materials/Dogme_Manifesto.htm">Dogme 95 manifesto</a> · <a href="https://ifsstech.files.wordpress.com/2008/06/the_vow_of_chastity.pdf">Vow of Chastity</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="bva8CdUB0a" vertical-align="center" background-color="palette-3"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="-ejvOIrL75" vertical-align="center"><p role="eyebrow">Section 03</p><h1 font-size="52pt" style="padding-right: 20%;">03 — Write your own vow</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 38%;">Turn the permission you protect into the rule that moves the work.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="DIVFtUUTOD" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="vv4WdcN698"><h2>Four steps, and you can run this on Monday</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="medium" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="YgWnENshwI" colspan="15"><h4>Name the default</h4><p>Write the version any competent studio would make. That is the thing you are trying to escape.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="M8pV1ja3iQ" colspan="15"><h4>Find the load-bearing freedom</h4><p>Identify the permission that makes the default possible. Usually budget, stock imagery or an unlimited palette.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="jPsY3xthCD" colspan="15"><h4>Remove it, and aim the hole</h4><p>Convert the removal into a focusing rule that says what you must now do.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Lx1sBK0Nmd" colspan="15"><h4>Set the dose and escape clause</h4><p>Use three to five rules. Name in advance the one condition under which you would break them.</p></grid-item></grid><h3 role="subheading">Five rules is a vow. Fifteen is a specification, and specifications produce compliance.</h3></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="whF4vzuN19"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="qtkzW-HFvH" vertical-align="center" colspan="38"><labels><label variant="solid">Fictional example</label></labels><h2>Worked example: a salon rebrand that does not exist</h2><p><b>This example is invented for this talk.</b> Fenwick &amp; Pike is a fictional salon; no client commissioned it, no work was delivered, and the visuals here are concept imagery, not case-study results.</p><p><b>The default:</b> soft neutral palette, serif wordmark, close-cropped model portraits and one script accent. Roughly every salon identity of the last decade.</p><p><b>The load-bearing freedom:</b> photography of people. Remove it and the whole default collapses.</p><p><b>The vow:</b> one typeface at two weights · two inks, no gradients · no photographs of people · every asset must survive reproduction at 30mm on a sticker.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="jYFVBeQ56Z" padding="0" colspan="22"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/3lCgWbAgYr8dr6vo8H5Tw.png" saved-media-id="gt7hw4i6ycncka0" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="practical-light-afterimage" alt="High-contrast editorial concept photograph of salon tools without people, comb teeth, folded foil and the curved silhouette of a shear arranged as bold flat graphic marks under one practical hard light, two-ink coral and bone palette on near-black, restrained grain, no text or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="iizF9A9gxo"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="DTsus9xEgC"><h2>What the rules forced</h2><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="LVdxMjTHHc" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="top-line" colspan="30"><h4>No people</h4><p>The identity moved toward objects of the trade: comb teeth, foil and the curve of a shear became flat graphic marks.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="g3k5bqOwTP" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="top-line" colspan="30"><h4>Two inks</h4><p>The soft gradient look died. A hard, high-contrast system emerged that survives a photocopier and a neon sign equally.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="O0xk4g0VTV" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="top-line" colspan="30"><h4>The 30mm sticker rule</h4><p>It vetoed the delicate wordmark in week one, which is exactly what a good constraint does early.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="spMtZ35Olj" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="top-line" colspan="30"><h4>One typeface</h4><p>Hierarchy moved into scale and spacing, which is cheaper to maintain and harder to get wrong.</p></grid-item></grid><h3 role="subheading">None of these were decisions. They were consequences. That is the point.</h3></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="hcU40Io7H9"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="VonPOZ72bv"><h2>How to tell your vow is wrong</h2><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="qOzY9zUoWd" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="side-line" colspan="30"><h4>It only says no</h4><p>A vow of pure prohibition is a filter, not a brief. Rewrite it toward the focusing form.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="9omLHQQLSa" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="side-line" colspan="30"><h4>Nothing has died yet</h4><p>If two weeks in nothing you liked has been vetoed, the rules are decorative.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="4YOj0m8Yg1" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="side-line" colspan="30"><h4>It governs meaning</h4><p>Method rules are generative. Rules about what the work may <i>say</i> are censorship in a better outfit.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="i-D3LL2Sb3" cell-style="subtle" cell-decor="side-line" colspan="30"><h4>Everyone is miserable and nothing ships</h4><p>That is futile effort. Loosen one dimension instead of pushing harder.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption"><a href="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/20413866231202031">Source: research on multidimensional constraints and futile effort</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="jTi3dZTXxt" vertical-align="end" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.58" card-margin-hidden="true"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Rxolk6OAp_KCGP6nJuxgi.png" saved-media-id="zqtqruzh6wpo3oh" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="practical-light-afterimage" alt="High-contrast editorial studio photograph of an anonymous hand removing one familiar creative tool from a precise arrangement on black paper, leaving a vivid coral and amber long-exposure afterimage in the empty space, one hard practical light, broad quiet near-black field behind the copy, restrained grain, no text or logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="jo-2aBgR5R" vertical-align="end"><h1 font-size="44pt" style="padding-right: 28%;">Pick the freedom you're most afraid to lose</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 30%;">The evidence points one way: constraints predict better creative performance, late ones especially, and the real work is tuning the dose rather than defending your options.</p><p style="padding-right: 30%;">Go back to the project sitting open on your desk. Find the permission you would defend hardest in a meeting. Take it away for two weeks and write down what happens.</p><p style="padding-right: 30%;">Bennett Cerf's fifty dollars bought the best-selling thing Geisel ever made. Your version costs less than that.</p></grid-item></grid></section>